 1 big thing: To live and buy in L.A. 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios Venture capitalist Josh Kushner and former Disney CEO Bob Iger are buying the NBA's Los Angeles Lakers at a record $12.5 billion valuation, Axios' Dan Primack reports. 

 😵‍💫 This is a dizzying turnaround for the Lakers, one of pro sports' most iconic franchises.

 Billionaire Mark Walter purchased a controlling stake just last October at a $10 billion valuation. Kushner and Iger, who work together on sports-focused investment platform Thrive Eternal, were first interested in an NBA expansion franchise in Las Vegas — but then turned their attention further west. 💸 A source says that Kushner and Iger each would invest individually, with Kushner serving as control owner.

 Thrive Eternal is expected to invest, but is limited by NBA rules to no more than a 20% stake. Expect other investors to join. Between the lines: Prosecutors are investigating Walter's insurance companies and investment firm, Bloomberg reported last month.

 What's next: The deal still requires league approval.

