 2. ⛽️ Diesel desperation 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: AAA. Chart: Ben Geman/Axios The global diesel market is getting even dicier amid the Iran and Russia-Ukraine wars, Axios' Ben Geman writes. 

 Diesel prices ripple through global economies, affecting shipping costs, construction and much more. 🤑 The average U.S. price is up 44 cents over the last month to $5.32 per gallon as of yesterday, per AAA. That's compared to $3.71 a year ago.

 The all-time high: $5.82, in June 2022. 📉 A new S&amp;P Global Energy analysis estimates that global refineries processed 7.5 million fewer barrels of crude oil daily last month than during the same period in 2025.

 A separate International Energy Agency report also shows a steep drop in crude processing. 🛢️ The U.S. has been exporting growing amounts of diesel to the thirsty global market, but S&amp;P says domestic refineries are running at 96% capacity — leaving little room to boost production.

 Mizuho Securities analyst Robert Yawger said in a note: "The situation is not going away anytime soon, and could very well get more expensive." Go deeper . 

 
 
